@begin(section)

@title(Command Reference)

This page documents every zick subcommand, its arguments, defaults, and
the result it returns.  Output is an NRDL document by default; use
@c(--set-output-format json) to get JSON instead.

@begin(section)
@title(How Options Are Gathered)

zick is a @link[uri="https://github.com/djha-skin/cliff"](CLIFF)-powered
program.  Every option can be given through three channels, in order of
precedence — configuration files, environment variables, then the command
line — with later values overriding earlier ones.

Configuration files live in three places, each overriding the one before it:

@begin(list)
@item(A system-wide file: @c(/etc/zick/config.nrdl) on Linux/POSIX,
@c(/Library/Preferences/zick/config.nrdl) on macOS, and
@c(%PROGRAMDATA%\zick\config.nrdl) on Windows)
@item(A per-user file: @c(${XDG_CONFIG_HOME}/zick/config.nrdl) — by
default @c(~/.config/zick/config.nrdl) — on Linux/POSIX,
@c(~/Library/Preferences/zick/config.nrdl) on macOS, and
@c(%LOCALAPPDATA%\zick\config.nrdl) on Windows)
@item(A project file named @c(.zick.nrdl) in the directory zick is run
from, or any of its parents)
@end(list)

Every option also has an environment variable, named
@c(ZICK_<KIND>_<OPTION>):

@begin(list)
@item(@c(ZICK_FLAG_<OPTION>=1) enables a flag)
@item(@c(ZICK_ITEM_<OPTION>=<VALUE>) sets a string value)
@item(@c(ZICK_LIST_<OPTION>=<V1>,<V2>,...) sets a list option)
@item(@c(ZICK_TABLE_<OPTION>=<K1>,<V1>=<K2>,<V2>=...) sets a key/value
table option)
@item(@c(ZICK_NRDL_<OPTION>=<NRDL_STRING>) sets a value using an NRDL
string)
@item(@c(ZICK_FILE_<OPTION>=<FILE_PATH>) sets a value from the contents
of an NRDL document in a file, as if by the @c(--file-*) flag)
@item(@c(ZICK_RAW_<OPTION>=<FILE_PATH>) sets a value from the raw bytes
of a file, as if by the @c(--raw-*) flag)
@end(list)

On the command line, options take a spelling that matches their kind:
@c(--enable-<option>) and @c(--disable-<option>) for flags,
@c(--set-<option> <value>) for strings, @c(--add-<option> <value>) for
lists, @c(--reset-<option>) to restore a default, and the
@c(--nrdl-*), @c(--file-*), and @c(--raw-*) spellings for structured
values.  Short aliases are listed per subcommand below.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Common Options)

Two options apply to every subcommand:

@begin(list)
@item(@c(-d)/@c(--set-start-directory) — the directory to start the
zick search from.  Defaults to the current working directory.  zick
walks up from here looking for the @c(.zick-db) marking directory that
names the project's database.)
@item(@c(--set-output-format) — @c("nrdl") — the default — or @c("json").
Controls the format of the emitted result document.)
@end(list)

The defaults for the remaining options are shown on the @c(zick help)
page under "The following options have been detected":
@c(cascade false), @c(download-package true), @c(dry-run false),
@c(output-format "nrdl").

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(init)

Initializes the zick database in the start directory: it creates the
@c(.zick-db) marking directory and the store file (@c(packages.nrdl)
inside it).  Commands run without @c(init) create the store on demand
the first time they open it.

@begin(list)
@item(Arguments: none beyond the common options)
@item(Aliases: none)
@end(list)

Result:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    result successful
    status successful
}
@end(code)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(add)

Downloads the zip archive at a URL, unpacks it into the project tree,
and records the package and the files it owns in the store.

@begin(list)
@item(@c(-k)/@c(--set-package-name) — the package name.  Required.)
@item(@c(-V)/@c(--set-package-version) — the package version.  Required.)
@item(@c(-l)/@c(--set-package-location) — the URL of the zip archive.
Required.)
@item(@c(-u)/@c(--add-package-dependency) — a dependency on another
installed package, by name.  May be given more than once.  The
dependee must already be installed.)
@item(@c(-m)/@c(--set-package-metadata) — a JSON string of extra
metadata, honored for its @c(zick) key: @c(config-files) — paths to
treat as configuration files — and @c(ghost-files) — paths to record
without writing.)
@item(@c(--json-download-authorizations) — a JSON string mapping hosts
to authorization records (@c(type) of @c("basic"), @c("header"), or
@c("oauth-token")), used when downloading the archive.)
@item(@c(-W)/@c(--disable-download-package) and
@c(-w)/@c(--enable-download-package) — whether to download and unpack
the archive.  Downloading is on by default; @c(-W) records the package
from its options alone, without touching the network or the tree.)
@item(@c(-n)/@c(--enable-insecure) and @c(-N)/@c(--disable-insecure) —
disable TLS certificate verification when downloading.)
@item(@c(-g)/@c(--enable-allow-downgrades) and
@c(-G)/@c(--disable-allow-downgrades) — allow re-adding a package at a
lower version than the one installed.  Off by default.)
@item(@c(-i)/@c(--enable-allow-inplace) and
@c(-I)/@c(--disable-allow-inplace) — allow replacing the currently
installed version with one that compares equal.  Off by default.)
@end(list)

Result (on success):

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    result successful
    status successful
}
@end(code)

zick signals an error when a dependency named by @c(-u) is not
installed, or when a file in the archive is already owned by another
package.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(files)

Lists the files owned by an installed package.

@begin(list)
@item(@c(-k)/@c(--set-package-name) — the package name.  Required.)
@end(list)

Result when the package is installed:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    package-files [
        {
            checksum "9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a3bf4f1b2b0b822c"
            class normal-file
            path "bin/tool"
            size 42
        }
    ]
    result package-found
    status successful
}
@end(code)

When the package is not installed, the result is
@c(result not-found) and @c(package-files) is omitted.  A package
recorded without downloading (e.g. with @c(-W)) has no files and also
reports @c(result not-found) until it is installed with files.  File
classes are @c(normal-file), @c(config-file), and @c(ghost-file).

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(info)

Shows information about an installed package: its name, version,
location, and metadata.

@begin(list)
@item(@c(-k)/@c(--set-package-name) — the package name.  Required.)
@end(list)

Result when the package is installed:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    package-information {
        location "https://example.com/mylib-1.2.0.zip"
        metadata {
        }
        name "mylib"
        version "1.2.0"
    }
    result package-found
    status successful
}
@end(code)

When the package is not installed, the result is
@c(result not-found).

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(dependers)

Lists the installed packages that depend on the named package.

@begin(list)
@item(@c(-k)/@c(--set-package-name) — the package name.  Required.)
@end(list)

Result when the package is installed:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    package-dependers [
        {
            location "https://example.com/myapp.zip"
            name "myapp"
            version "0.1.0"
        }
    ]
    result package-found
    status successful
}
@end(code)

When the package is not installed, the result is
@c(result not-found).  An empty list of dependers renders as
@c(package-dependers [ ]).

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(dependees)

Lists the packages the named package depends on.

@begin(list)
@item(@c(-k)/@c(--set-package-name) — the package name.  Required.)
@end(list)

The result shape mirrors @c(dependers), with the key
@c(package-dependees) instead of @c(package-dependers).

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(freeze)

Writes the installed package set to @c(zick.lock.nrdl), a versioned NRDL
lockfile intended to be committed with the project.  Entries contain the
package name, version, archive location, dependency names, and metadata, and
are sorted by name for deterministic output.  The project configuration file
@c(.zick.nrdl) remains separate.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(sync)

Reads @c(zick.lock.nrdl) and installs missing or changed packages in
dependency-first order.  Packages whose name, version, location, and
dependency edges already match are skipped.  Packages installed locally but
absent from the lockfile are reported as @c(extra-packages), but are not
removed automatically.  @c(-r)/@c(--enable-dry-run) reports the
synchronization without downloading or modifying the project.  Unsupported lockfile versions, duplicate names, and malformed fields are
rejected before installation begins.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(remove)

Uninstalls a package: deletes the files it owns (backing up its
configuration files) and removes its record from the store.

@begin(list)
@item(@c(-k)/@c(--set-package-name) — the package name.  Required.)
@item(@c(-c)/@c(--enable-cascade) and @c(-C)/@c(--disable-cascade) —
with @c(-c), also remove the packages that depend on the named one,
sinks first.  Off by default; without it, removing a package that
others depend on signals an error.)
@item(@c(-r)/@c(--enable-dry-run) and @c(-R)/@c(--disable-dry-run) —
with @c(-r), compute and report the removal without touching the tree
or the store.)
@end(list)

Result when the package is installed:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    dry-run false
    removed-packages [
        {
            location "https://example.com/myapp.zip"
            name "myapp"
            version "0.1.0"
        }
    ]
    result package-found
    status successful
}
@end(code)

With @c(-c), @c(removed-packages) lists every removed package, sinks
first.  When the package is not installed, the result is
@c(result not-found).

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(list)

Lists the installed packages, sorted by name.

No arguments beyond the common options.

Result:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    packages [
        {
            location "https://example.com/mylib-1.2.0.zip"
            metadata {
            }
            name "mylib"
            version "1.2.0"
        }
    ]
    result successful
    status successful
}
@end(code)

When nothing is installed, zick prints nothing and exits 0.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(orphans)

Lists the orphaned packages: the installed packages that nothing
depends on (the source nodes of the dependency graph).

No arguments beyond the common options.

Result:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    orphaned-packages [
    ]
    result successful
    status successful
}
@end(code)

An empty store renders @c(orphaned-packages [ ]) — never
@c(false) — because the collection is emitted as an empty sequence.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(verify)

Checks the files of an installed package against the store: size and
SHA-256 checksum for @c(normal-file) records, presence for
@c(config-file) records.  Ghost files are expected to be absent.

@begin(list)
@item(@c(-k)/@c(--set-package-name) — the package name.  Required.)
@end(list)

Result when every file checks out:

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    result package-found
    status successful
    verification-results [ ]
}
@end(code)

When verification finds problems, zick exits 4 and reports each
problem class under @c(verification-results):

@begin[lang=nrdl](code)
{
    result package-found
    status verification-failed
    verification-results {
        checksum-discrepancy [
            {
                path "bin/tool"
                source-checksum "9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a"
                target-checksum "a3bf4f1b2b0b822c2ad5023b9c5b7f8d1a"
            }
        ]
        file-missing [
            {
                path "lib/mylib.lisp"
            }
        ]
    }
}
@end(code)

The possible result keys are @c(file-missing),
@c(size-discrepancy), @c(checksum-discrepancy),
@c(config-file-missing), @c(path-not-file), and
@c(path-not-directory).

When the package is not installed, zick exits 3 with
@c(status package-not-found).

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(help)

@c(zick help) — or @c(-h)/@c(--help) — prints the CLIFF help page: how
options are gathered, the alias table, the detected defaults, and the
list of subcommands.  @c(zick) with no subcommand prints a short usage
summary instead.

@end(section)

@end(section)
